## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of memory hierarchy and cache effects, which are crucial for writing high-performance, low-latency code in systems programming.

## Key Concepts
- **False sharing**: When two threads modify variables that share the same cache line, even if they access different variables, the cache coherence protocol forces invalidation and reloads
- **Cache lines**: Typically 64 bytes on modern CPUs; data is transferred between main memory and CPU caches in these blocks
- **MESI protocol**: Cache coherence protocol (Modified, Exclusive, Shared, Invalid) that ensures consistency but introduces overhead when false sharing occurs
- **Memory alignment and padding**: Restructuring data to occupy separate cache lines eliminates false sharing at the cost of increased memory usage
- **pthreads and barriers**: Lightweight threading with synchronization primitives to coordinate threads and measure timing accurately

## Code Example
```c
// Variant A: no padding (false sharing occurs)
typedef struct { long v0; long v1; } counters_t;

// Variant B: 64-byte padding per counter (no false sharing)
typedef struct { 
    long v0; 
    char pad0[64 - sizeof(long)]; 
    long v1; 
    char pad1[64 - sizeof(long)]; 
} counters_padded_t;
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of crash-consistent data structures, persistent memory programming, and low-latency recovery mechanisms in real-world systems, connecting hardware flush instructions to correct ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Non-volatile memory semantics and persistence instructions (clwb, sfence)
- Write-ahead logging protocols and commit/rollback mechanisms for crash safety
- Epoch-based reclamation and idempotent recovery procedures
- Log replay and in-memory index reconstruction after power loss
- NUMA-aware memory layout and efficient log space management

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch of log append and commit protocol
typedef struct {
    uint64_t key;
    uint64_t val;
    uint64_t epoch;
    uint8_t committed; // 0=pending, 1=committed
} log_entry_t;

// Put operation with crash consistency
int kv_put(uint64_t key, uint64_t val) {
    log_entry_t *entry = alloc_log_entry();
    entry->key = key;
    entry->val = val;
    entry->epoch = get_current_epoch();
    entry->committed = 0; // pending

    _mm_clwb(entry); // flush to NVRAM
    _mm_sfence();    // ensure ordering

    entry->committed = 1; // mark committed
    _mm_clwb(&entry->committed);
    _mm_sfence();

    update_index(key, val); // update in-memory index
    return 0;
}
```
